{"reason":"compiler-message","package_id":"path+file:///C:/Users/koosh/Dev/Civis-cxA/crates/engine#civ-engine@0.1.0","manifest_path":"C:\\Users\\koosh\\Dev\\Civis-cxA\\crates\\engine\\Cargo.toml","target":{"kind":["lib"],"crate_types":["lib"],"name":"civ_engine","src_path":"C:\\Users\\koosh\\Dev\\Civis-cxA\\crates\\engine\\src\\lib.rs","edition":"2021","doc":true,"doctest":true,"test":true},"message":{"rendered":"error[E0277]: cannot subtract-assign `&mut FixedI32<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UTerm, B1>, B0>, B0>, B0>, B0>>` from `FixedI32<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UTerm, B1>, B0>, B0>, B0>, B0>>`\n   --> crates\\engine\\src\\disasters.rs:356:11\n    |\n356 |     *pool -= spent;\n    |           ^^ no implementation for `FixedI32<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UTerm, B1>, B0>, B0>, B0>, B0>> -= &mut FixedI32<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UTerm, B1>, B0>, B0>, B0>, B0>>`\n    |\n    = help: the trait `SubAssign<&mut FixedI32<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UTerm, B1>, B0>, B0>, B0>, B0>>>` is not implemented for `FixedI32<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UTerm, B1>, B0>, B0>, B0>, B0>>`\nhelp: consider dereferencing here\n    |\n356 |     *pool -= *spent;\n    |              +\n\n","$message_type":"diagnostic","children":[{"children":[],"code":null,"level":"help","message":"the trait `SubAssign<&mut FixedI32<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UTerm, B1>, B0>, B0>, B0>, B0>>>` is not implemented for `FixedI32<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UTerm, B1>, B0>, B0>, B0>, B0>>`","rendered":null,"spans":[]},{"children":[],"code":null,"level":"help","message":"consider dereferencing here","rendered":null,"spans":[{"byte_end":14305,"byte_start":14305,"column_end":14,"column_start":14,"expansion":null,"file_name":"crates\\engine\\src\\disasters.rs","is_primary":true,"label":null,"line_end":356,"line_start":356,"suggested_replacement":"*","suggestion_applicability":"MachineApplicable","text":[{"highlight_end":14,"highlight_start":14,"text":"    *pool -= spent;"}]}]}],"level":"error","message":"cannot subtract-assign `&mut FixedI32<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UTerm, B1>, B0>, B0>, B0>, B0>>` from `FixedI32<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UTerm, B1>, B0>, B0>, B0>, B0>>`","spans":[{"byte_end":14304,"byte_start":14302,"column_end":13,"column_start":11,"expansion":null,"file_name":"crates\\engine\\src\\disasters.rs","is_primary":true,"label":"no implementation for `FixedI32<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UTerm, B1>, B0>, B0>, B0>, B0>> -= &mut FixedI32<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UInt<UTerm, B1>, B0>, B0>, B0>, B0>>`","line_end":356,"line_start":356,"suggested_replacement":null,"suggestion_applicability":null,"text":[{"highlight_end":13,"highlight_start":11,"text":"    *pool -= spent;"}]}],"code":{"code":"E0277","explanation":"You tried to use a type which doesn't implement some trait in a place which\nexpected that trait.\n\nErroneous code example:\n\n```compile_fail,E0277\n// here we declare the Foo trait with a bar method\ntrait Foo {\n    fn bar(&self);\n}\n\n// we now declare a function which takes an object implementing the Foo trait\nfn some_func<T: Foo>(foo: T) {\n    foo.bar();\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    // we now call the method with the i32 type, which doesn't implement\n    // the Foo trait\n    some_func(5i32); // error: the trait bound `i32 : Foo` is not satisfied\n}\n```\n\nIn order to fix this error, verify that the type you're using does implement\nthe trait. Example:\n\n```\ntrait Foo {\n    fn bar(&self);\n}\n\n// we implement the trait on the i32 type\nimpl Foo for i32 {\n    fn bar(&self) {}\n}\n\nfn some_func<T: Foo>(foo: T) {\n    foo.bar(); // we can now use this method since i32 implements the\n               // Foo trait\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    some_func(5i32); // ok!\n}\n```\n\nOr in a generic context, an erroneous code example would look like:\n\n```compile_fail,E0277\nfn some_func<T>(foo: T) {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", foo); // error: the trait `core::fmt::Debug` is not\n                           //        implemented for the type `T`\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    // We now call the method with the i32 type,\n    // which *does* implement the Debug trait.\n    some_func(5i32);\n}\n```\n\nNote that the error here is in the definition of the generic function. Although\nwe only call it with a parameter that does implement `Debug`, the compiler\nstill rejects the function. It must work with all possible input types. In\norder to make this example compile, we need to restrict the generic type we're\naccepting:\n\n```\nuse std::fmt;\n\n// Restrict the input type to types that implement Debug.\nfn some_func<T: fmt::Debug>(foo: T) {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", foo);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    // Calling the method is still fine, as i32 implements Debug.\n    some_func(5i32);\n\n    // This would fail to compile now:\n    // struct WithoutDebug;\n    // some_func(WithoutDebug);\n}\n```\n\nRust only looks at the signature of the called function, as such it must\nalready specify all requirements that will be used for every type parameter.\n"}}}
